    "title": "Haven't filed your taxes yet? Here's how to request a tax extension",
    "text": "April 15, 2026, 10:05 a.m. CTThe 2026 federal tax deadline has arrived and, somehow, you haven’t filed your income tax returns.It’s OK. Life happens.If you’re not able to get your taxes done or have someone file them for you by April 15, there’s still one option left.You can request an extension to file your federal tax return from the Internal Revenue Service. However, you’ll have to do so quickly.Here’s everything you need to know.How to file a tax extensionTo request a tax extension from the IRS, you’ll have to fill out Form 4868.This form can be completed and filed electronically using your tax software or by hiring a tax professional to do it for you.You can also pay all or part of your estimated income tax due and tell the IRS that the submitted payment is for an extension using your bank account. You can also make the payment using Click to Pay, PayPal, Venmo, a debit card or a credit card.The third option to request an extension is to do it the old-fashioned way. You can mail Form 4868 to the IRS. You can also include your estimated tax payment when you mail it.If you’re filing the extension by mail, it must be postmarked by the tax deadline, which is April 15. In fact, any request for a tax extension must be done by April 15.A federal tax extension cannot be requested after April 15.How long is the tax extension?Tax extensions allow the taxpayer six months to file their federal income tax return.However, the tax extension does not extend the deadline for money owed to the IRS. Any taxes due to the IRS are due by the tax deadline.The IRS also makes exceptions to the deadline for citizens who are out of the country.What reasons can I apply for a federal tax extension?In many cases, taxpayers may apply for a tax extension if they are missing tax information.Taxpayers may also request an extension after dealing with major life events like marriage, divorce, a new child, death in the family or a natural disaster.Some taxpayers even request an extension due to complex issues involving their tax return like acknowledging foreign income or issues regarding opening or closing a small business.Does the state of Iowa also allow tax extensions?You automatically have more time to complete your Iowa income taxes. The state's deadline is April 30. But, if you need even more time, the Iowa Department of Revenue does offer a six-month extension to file state income tax returns.However, taxpayers do not have to fill out a form to receive the extension. Instead, taxpayers are required to pay at least 90% of their total tax liability in order to receive the extension from the Iowa Department of Revenue.You can find more details about the state tax extension at revenue.iowa.gov.Is there a penalty for not paying your taxes?Absolutely.The IRS charges a penalty of 0.5% of the unpaid taxes each month or part of a month the taxes are unpaid.The IRS does not penalize more than 25% of your unpaid taxes.The penalty for not filing an individual federal tax return is 5% of the tax due for each month or part of a month the return is late. Again, the IRS does not penalize more than 25% for this infraction.Emery Glover is an audience engagement specialist for the Des Moines Register. Reach him at eglover@usatodayco.com.",
